nss_hal: park the core before copying the firmware over it

A warm reboot does not reset the NSS block: the previous kernel's
firmware keeps executing out of the load region, and
nss_hal_firmware_load() copies the new image straight over it. The
new firmware then comes up dead (no first interrupt) or mute.

Assert the core-local reset clamp before the copy, exactly like
nss_hal_remove() already does for the rmmod path. core_reset() releases
the clamp in its normal boot sequence. The GCC block resets are left
alone: on IPQ5018 they gate hardware shared with the host GMAC and
asserting them kills ethernet past the point a hard reset helps.

--- a/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
+++ b/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
@@ -137,6 +137,18 @@
 	  nss_hal_firmware_info(nss_dev, nss_fw);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A warm reboot does not reset the NSS block, so the previous
+	 * kernel's firmware may still be executing out of this very region.
+	 * Park the core behind its local reset clamp before replacing its
+	 * instruction memory; core_reset() releases the clamp in its usual
+	 * sequence later. The clamp is core-local: the GCC block resets must
+	 * NOT be touched here, on IPQ5018 they gate hardware shared with the
+	 * host GMAC.
+	 */
+	if (npd->nmap)
+		nss_write_32(npd->nmap, NSS_REGS_RESET_CTRL_OFFSET, 0x1);
+
 	dev_info(&nss_dev->dev, "fw of size %d bytes copied to addr: %x, nss_id: %d\n", (int)nss_fw->size, npd->load_addr, nss_ctx->id);
 	memcpy_toio(load_mem, nss_fw->data, nss_fw->size);
 	release_firmware(nss_fw);
